<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hoffmann von Fallersleben</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben was a German poet and scholar of early German literature. In 1841, on Heligoland, he wrote the 'Lied der Deutschen', whose third stanza is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Dismissed from his professorship for his liberal views, he spent his last years as librarian of the ducal library at Corvey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoffmann-von-fallersleben.jpg" alt="Hoffmann von Fallersleben" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographisches Atelier von W(ilhelm) Halffter, Berlin, Leipziger-Strasse 91, Ecke Mauerstraße · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoffmann_von_Fallersleben,_Carte-de-visite_von_W._Halffter,_Berlin,_Bild-_und_R%C3%BCckseite_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1841, while on holiday on the North Sea island of Heligoland, then British, the poet and linguist <b>August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben</b> wrote &quot;Das Lied der Deutschen&quot; as a new text for Haydn's tune.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hoffmann von Fallersleben</b> took it into his collection of Silesian folk songs in 1842, as a song from Neukirch bei Schönau, and his version became the most popular.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-gedanken-sind-frei">Die Gedanken sind frei</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1841 <b>Hoffmann von Fallersleben</b> wrote &quot;Das Lied der Deutschen&quot; to Haydn's melody, and its third stanza is today the national anthem of Germany.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Hoffmann von Fallersleben</b>, author of the German national anthem, later worked in its library.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-corvey">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fallersleben, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Princely Abbey of Corvey. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.42,10.72&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Fallersleben on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4791 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hoffmann von Fallersleben"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5241 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5241 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.42,10.72&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
